  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945897/assets/4c19425664f79df1f46863f59268ebd27e20defce41b21e877926bbcadc9e5c3.png)\n\nConsider the triangles $\u25b3ABC$ and $\u25b3CDE$ shown above. If the area of $\u25b3CDE$ is $42{\\text{\u00a0cm}}^{2}$, find the area of $\u25b3ABC.$\n\n(Figure not drawn to scale)",
  "worked_solution": "Let's consider $\u25b3CDE.$ We can see that $\\overset{\u2015}{CD}$ is the height corresponding to the base $\\overset{\u2015}{DE}$ and that $DE=7\\text{\u00a0cm}.$ \n\nSince the area of $\u25b3CDE$ is $42{\\text{\u00a0cm}}^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\ 42 & =\\frac{7\\cdot CD}{2} \\\\ 7CD & =84 \\\\ CD & =12\\text{\u00a0cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that $\\overset{\u2015}{CD}$ is also the height of $\u25b3ABC$ corresponding to the base $\\overset{\u2015}{AB}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945897/assets/0014faf05eaf15d70f885802e5b820b0c72d42a96f400442f827ed229de8b240.png)\n\nTherefore, we find the area of $\u25b3ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{AB\\cdot CD}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot 12}{2} \\\\  & =30{\\text{\u00a0cm}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
